      PracticeQuestion(
        scenario:
            "Question 16: (a) State four acceptable ways of saving money.\n"
            "(b) Explain four reasons for which it is wrong to give a bribe.\n"
            "(BECE 2023 Q8)",
        answer: "Correct Answer: (a) Answer:\n"
            "⦿ Using a secure money box — Placing spare cash into a personal box at home to build up funds for future needs.\n"
            "⦿ Participating in structured Susu schemes — Depositing daily earnings with trusted non-bank financial collectors for safe keeping.\n"
            "⦿ Investing in secure public assets — Allocating capital into government treasury bills, bonds, or real estate like family land or houses.\n"
            "⦿ Depositing money in bank accounts — Opening interest-yielding savings or current accounts with licensed commercial banks.\n"
            "⦿ Purchasing corporate market shares — Buying stock shares in viable companies to secure co-ownership rights and earn regular dividend payouts.\n"
            "⦿ Joining local credit unions — Saving funds with community credit groups to secure low-interest institutional loans when needed.\n"
            "⦿ Lending money to trusted individuals — Providing financial support to reliable people under clear agreements that return a steady interest profit.\n"
            "⦿ Depositing funds into mobile money wallets — Keeping financial balances stored securely inside digital networks like MTN or Vodafone accounts.\n\n"
            "Answer: (b)\n"
            "⦿ Violates scriptural commands — Bribery contradicts sacred laws, which warn that accepting a bribe can blind judgment and distort the truth.\n"
            "⦿ Breaches statutory state laws — Bribery is a serious criminal offense under state legal codes, exposing guilty parties to prosecution and imprisonment.\n"
            "⦿ Leads to the misuse of public resources — Bribery can compromise administrative integrity, leading officials to divert public funds into unproductive projects.\n"
            "⦿ Undermines structural development — Bypassing legal and professional processes can result in low-quality work and squandered government revenues on poorly executed infrastructure.\n"
            "⦿ Discourages an active work ethic — Allowing people to buy jobs or services through under-the-table payments undermines the value of hard work and merit.\n"
            "⦿ Undermines personal integrity — Relying on bribery encourages dishonest habits, prompting people to discard moral standards for quick financial gain.\n"
            "⦿ Widens the social wealth gap — Bribery allows affluent individuals to control public services, deepening poverty for the broader community.\n"
            "⦿ Destroys trust in public institutions — Uncovering corruption among public officials erodes public respect and confidence in state leadership.\n"
            "⦿ Brings personal and family disgrace — Getting caught in bribery can ruin an individual's reputation, leading to social shame and isolation.\n"
            "⦿ Leads to hiring unqualified staff — Bribery can result in hiring unqualified candidates for critical public roles, which lowers productivity and wastes state resources.",
        explanation: ".",
